{{> judge-opening}}

JUDGMENT POINT: list the terms of the Strand claims in the input -- `strand_claims` -- that THIS ARTICLE WILL NOT KEEP, each with the plain phrase the article uses in its place. A Strand carries the source's own technical terms and expressions; a term the article keeps is used as served and has no entry, and a term the adopted Thesis -- `thesis`, the article's title -- carries is always kept. A term is one or more words exactly as they occur in a claim, in the form an `introduces` entry's `term` takes. `as` is written in `language`, the article's one language. The Brief's Defined Terms ride in `defined_terms`: a term classified `defined` is one the article gives a sense, and a term the article keeps to define is not rendered. An empty list is an answer: the article keeps every served word.
REQUIRED RECORD SHAPE: ONE record AT ITS OWN ROOT -- `version` "1" and `renderings`, a list of `{ term, as }` entries, as src/candidate-schema.json's `renderings_record` declares
WHAT IS REFUSED: a record whose `version` is not "1"; `renderings` not a list; an entry that is not a `{ term, as }` map, carries any other key, carries no term or no `as`, or renders a term as itself; a term listed twice; a term that occurs whole-word in no Strand claim; a term the Thesis carries. The next Call receives the refused record beside its refusal, both verbatim (kogaki#1438).

